"""Week45 Days 5-6: tree regressors and a fully traceable demand experiment."""

L(4, '1. Predict numbers with a decision tree',
  'Explain threshold branches, leaf predictions and squared-error tree fitting.',
  [('DecisionTreeRegressor; leaf prediction', 'A regression tree learns feature-threshold splits. Under its default squared-error criterion, each terminal leaf predicts the mean training target of its assigned rows.'),
   ('criterion; min_samples_leaf', 'criterion defines split quality; the default is squared_error here. min_samples_leaf requires a minimum number of training samples per final leaf and controls one aspect of flexibility.')],
  'A regression tree partitions the input space using questions such as guests <= a learned threshold. A new row follows the branches and receives a terminal leaf’s numeric prediction. This differs from fitting one global straight line and from counting class votes in a classification tree.\n\nWith the default squared-error criterion, a leaf’s prediction is the mean target among its training members. Split thresholds and leaf values are learned from training, not from held-out answers. Different criteria can change those rules, so state which configuration you use.\n\nThe tiny example uses two target levels to make the threshold and mean visible. It is an interface demonstration, not a useful demand evaluation. Trees can fit nonlinear and interaction patterns without manually supplying squared products, but unrestricted branching can memorise noise. They also do not automatically solve leakage or validate input units. The next part inspects depth and step-like predictions.',
  'from sklearn.tree import DecisionTreeRegressor\nX, y = [[1.0], [2.0], [3.0], [4.0]], [10.0, 12.0, 30.0, 32.0]\nm = DecisionTreeRegressor(max_depth=1, random_state=45).fit(X, y)\nprint("Criterion:", m.criterion)\nprint("Learned threshold:", round(float(m.tree_.threshold[0]), 3))\nprint("Predictions:", m.predict([[1.5], [3.5]]).tolist())',
  'Fit a single-leaf tree using min_samples_split larger than the training count and verify its mean prediction.',
  'from sklearn.tree import DecisionTreeRegressor\nm = DecisionTreeRegressor(min_samples_split=10, random_state=45).fit([[1], [2], [3]], [10.0, 20.0, 60.0])\nprint("Leaves:", m.get_n_leaves())\nprint("Mean prediction:", m.predict([[100]]).round(3).tolist())',
  'Inspect the learned leaf id for two new inputs.',
  'from sklearn.tree import DecisionTreeRegressor\nm = DecisionTreeRegressor(max_depth=1, random_state=45).fit([[1], [2], [3], [4]], [10.0, 12.0, 30.0, 32.0])\nprint("Leaf ids:", m.apply([[1.5], [3.5]]).tolist())',
  [('What does a leaf predict with the default squared-error regression criterion?', 'Mean training target of that leaf', 'The globally largest label', 'A class probability automatically', 'The terminal value minimises squared error for its assigned training cases.'),
   ('Where are split thresholds learned?', 'Training data', 'Final test labels', 'Only from the feature names', 'The tree is a fitted estimator with training-derived branches.')],
  'Treat a regression tree’s leaf value as a guaranteed future amount.', 'Explain the training mean and evaluate predictions on relevant held-out cases.',
  'The tree separates the two low and two high targets at 2.5. The leaf means are 11 and 31, which become the predictions for the two example rows.')

L(4, '2. Control depth and recognise step-like extrapolation',
  'Compare a shallow and flexible tree and inspect what they predict between observations.',
  [('Tree depth; piecewise-constant prediction', 'Depth limits the number of branch decisions. A regression tree predicts a constant within each final region rather than extending a fitted linear slope.'),
   ('Tree extrapolation limit', 'Outside observed feature ranges, ordinary tree leaves generally continue their existing constant predictions; they do not infer a new numerical trend.')],
  'A deeper tree can create more specialised regions, often reducing training error. That flexibility can also follow noise. min_samples_leaf and max_depth offer different controls; neither is a guarantee of the best unseen behaviour. Compare on development cases.\n\nThe chart uses one synthetic numeric input so the step boundaries are visible. An unrestricted tree can track many training values, while a shallow tree uses broader regions. Outside the observed range, both continue an edge leaf rather than extend the smooth simulator’s trend. This matters for demand levels or feature combinations absent from training.\n\nDo not infer that a tree is always worse than a line for extrapolation: a line can extrapolate implausibly too. The correct lesson is to understand each estimator’s behaviour and intended input support. The examples use separate local development data; the final project test remains reserved.',
  'import numpy as np\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nfrom sklearn.tree import DecisionTreeRegressor\nfrom sklearn.metrics import mean_absolute_error\nrng = np.random.default_rng(45)\nX = np.linspace(-2, 2, 24).reshape(-1, 1)\ny = 20 + 8 * np.sin(X[:, 0]) + rng.normal(0, 2, len(X))\ngrid = np.linspace(-3, 3, 240).reshape(-1, 1)\nfig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(6.5, 4))\nax.scatter(X[:, 0], y, label="Training rows", s=20, color="#333333")\nfor depth in [2, None]:\n    m = DecisionTreeRegressor(max_depth=depth, random_state=45).fit(X, y)\n    print("Depth", depth, "leaves", m.get_n_leaves(), "training MAE", round(mean_absolute_error(y, m.predict(X)), 3))\n    ax.plot(grid[:, 0], m.predict(grid), label=f"Depth {depth}")\nax.axvspan(-2, 2, color="#cccccc", alpha=0.15, label="Observed input range")\nax.set(xlabel="Teaching input", ylabel="Simulated response", title="Tree predictions form constant regions")\nax.legend(fontsize=8)\nfig.tight_layout()\nfig.savefig("chart.png", dpi=110)\nplt.close(fig)',
  'Compare leaf counts when min_samples_leaf is1 versus4 on the same training table.',
  'import numpy as np\nfrom sklearn.tree import DecisionTreeRegressor\nX = np.arange(16).reshape(-1, 1)\ny = np.sin(X[:, 0])\nfor minimum in [1, 4]:\n    m = DecisionTreeRegressor(min_samples_leaf=minimum, random_state=45).fit(X, y)\n    print("minimum", minimum, "leaves", m.get_n_leaves())',
  'Show that an ordinary tree gives the same edge leaf prediction at two far-above-range values.',
  'from sklearn.tree import DecisionTreeRegressor\nm = DecisionTreeRegressor(random_state=45).fit([[1], [2], [3]], [10.0, 20.0, 30.0])\nprint("Above-range predictions:", m.predict([[10], [100]]).tolist())',
  [('Does a standard regression tree continue a linear slope beyond the training range?', 'No; it follows an existing leaf region', 'Always', 'Only when max_depth is None', 'The prediction is piecewise constant under this model.'),
   ('Does a deeper tree guarantee lower unseen error?', 'No', 'Always', 'Only with more leaves', 'Training fit and generalisation need separate evidence.')],
  'Expect unrestricted depth to guarantee the best model and extrapolate any new demand trend.', 'Control complexity, inspect support and compare development errors.',
  'The chart’s shaded interval marks where training inputs exist. The constant edge predictions show why a tree cannot invent a slope beyond that region, even when its training fit is very close.',
  figure=('Shallow and unrestricted tree regression steps inside and outside the observed input interval', 'The shaded region contains the synthetic training inputs. The displayed training errors are not held-out scores; the step continuations expose each tree’s extrapolation behaviour.'))

L(4, '3. Random forests average varied training trees',
  'Explain bootstrap sampling, tree averaging and the limits of ensemble stability.',
  [('RandomForestRegressor; bagging', 'A forest averages predictions from many fitted trees. Bootstrap row samples and configurable feature subsampling create variation between trees.'),
   ('n_estimators; max_features; n_jobs', 'n_estimators chooses tree count; max_features controls feature candidates at a split. n_jobs=1 keeps these teaching runs sequential and reproducible within the declared environment.')],
  'A single flexible tree can depend strongly on which training rows were sampled. A forest fits many varied trees and averages their numerical predictions. Averaging can reduce variability, but it does not remove systematic data errors, leakage or a mismatched future population.\n\nThe example explicitly sets bootstrap=True and max_features=0.8. With few columns, the actual feature count follows the estimator’s integer rule. The project’s predeclared forest uses default feature availability plus bootstrap row variation; record configurations rather than assuming all forests use identical randomness.\n\nMore trees generally increase computation and can stabilise the average, but tree count alone is not an automatic solution to overfitting or bias. Depth, leaf size and data quality still matter. Compare validation predictions on the same cases and metric. A forest also inherits tree-style limits on extrapolating new numerical trends.',
  DEMAND_DEV + 'from sklearn.ensemble import RandomForestRegressor\nfrom sklearn.metrics import mean_absolute_error\nm = RandomForestRegressor(n_estimators=40, max_depth=8, min_samples_leaf=3, max_features=0.8, bootstrap=True, random_state=45, n_jobs=1).fit(Xt, yt)\nprint("Tree count:", len(m.estimators_))\nprint("Validation MAE:", round(mean_absolute_error(yv, m.predict(Xv)), 3))\nprint("Individual first-row predictions:", [round(float(tree.predict(Xv.to_numpy()[:1])[0]), 2) for tree in m.estimators_[:3]])',
  'Verify that a forest prediction equals the average of its component tree predictions.',
  DEMAND_DEV + 'import numpy as np\nfrom sklearn.ensemble import RandomForestRegressor\nm = RandomForestRegressor(n_estimators=8, random_state=45, n_jobs=1).fit(Xt, yt)\nrow = Xv.iloc[:1]\nparts = [tree.predict(row.to_numpy())[0] for tree in m.estimators_]\nprint("Mean of trees:", round(float(np.mean(parts)), 6))\nprint("Forest prediction:", round(float(m.predict(row)[0]), 6))',
  'Repeat a fixed-seed forest fit and compare predictions in this environment.',
  DEMAND_DEV + 'import numpy as np\nfrom sklearn.ensemble import RandomForestRegressor\na = RandomForestRegressor(n_estimators=8, random_state=45, n_jobs=1).fit(Xt, yt)\nb = RandomForestRegressor(n_estimators=8, random_state=45, n_jobs=1).fit(Xt, yt)\nprint("Repeat predictions:", bool(np.allclose(a.predict(Xv), b.predict(Xv))))',
  [('How does this forest combine tree regression predictions?', 'Averages their numeric predictions', 'Takes only the first tree', 'Uses a classification majority label', 'Regression forest aggregation is numerical averaging.'),
   ('Can averaging trees repair a feature containing the future target?', 'No', 'Always', 'Only with many trees', 'An ensemble can exploit the same leaked information as a single estimator.')],
  'Many trees make leakage, unit errors and future population changes irrelevant.', 'Treat the ensemble as a declared model with the same data and evaluation responsibilities.',
  'The individual predictions vary because their trees were fitted from varied training samples and feature choices. The challenge directly checks the averaging operation rather than relying on the model name.')

L(4, '4. Gradient boosting builds successive corrections',
  'Distinguish sequential boosting from a forest’s averaging of varied trees.',
  [('GradientBoostingRegressor; learning_rate', 'Boosting adds successive fitted corrections to a current prediction. learning_rate shrinks each stage’s contribution; it interacts with the number and complexity of stages.'),
   ('staged_predict; squared-error corrections', 'staged_predict exposes predictions after each stage. With squared-error loss, successive trees fit residual-like negative gradients on the training data.')],
  'A random forest averages varied trees; gradient boosting builds an additive sequence intended to correct the current model. For squared-error loss, a stage fits residual-like errors, then adds a scaled correction. This sequential relationship is why learning rate and stage count matter together.\n\nThe example uses shallow trees, an explicit learning rate and fixed stage count on the separate development table. It prints validation MAE after selected stages. Validation performance can eventually stop improving or worsen even while training fit improves; adding stages is a complexity choice, not a promise of endless gains.\n\nDo not tune stage count repeatedly against the final project test. Predeclare candidates or use an appropriate development procedure. Different boosting algorithms have different handling and settings; this lesson describes the classic GradientBoostingRegressor used here, not every library called boosting.',
  DEMAND_DEV + 'from sklearn.ensemble import GradientBoostingRegressor\nfrom sklearn.metrics import mean_absolute_error\nm = GradientBoostingRegressor(n_estimators=60, learning_rate=0.1, max_depth=2, random_state=45).fit(Xt, yt)\nfor stage, predicted in enumerate(m.staged_predict(Xv), 1):\n    if stage in [1, 10, 30, 60]:\n        print("Stage", stage, "validation MAE", round(mean_absolute_error(yv, predicted), 3))',
  'Verify that the final staged prediction matches ordinary predict.',
  DEMAND_DEV + 'import numpy as np\nfrom sklearn.ensemble import GradientBoostingRegressor\nm = GradientBoostingRegressor(n_estimators=12, max_depth=2, random_state=45).fit(Xt, yt)\nlast = list(m.staged_predict(Xv))[-1]\nprint("Final stage matches:", bool(np.allclose(last, m.predict(Xv))))',
  'Record a predeclared learning-rate/stage configuration and its development role.',
  'plan = {"learning_rate": 0.1, "n_estimators": 60, "max_depth": 2, "evaluation": "development first"}\nprint(plan)',
  [('How does boosting differ from the forest described earlier?', 'It adds successive corrections to the current prediction', 'It always trains only one tree', 'It needs final test answers for each stage', 'The additive sequential structure differs from averaging varied parallel trees.'),
   ('Must validation error improve at every added stage?', 'No', 'Always', 'Only when learning_rate is positive', 'Additional capacity can fit sample-specific noise.')],
  'Keep adding boosting stages until the final test score is best.', 'Treat rate, depth and stage count as development choices and preserve final evaluation.',
  'Each printed score uses the same validation rows at a selected stage of one training fit. No stage is fitted on those validation labels.')

L(4, '5. Read feature importance with its limitations',
  'Contrast training impurity importance with validation permutation importance.',
  [('feature_importances_; impurity importance', 'Tree ensembles can summarise training split improvements by feature. These values can favour features with many possible splits and are not causal effects.'),
   ('permutation_importance', 'Shuffle one feature on evaluation rows and measure the loss of a chosen score. With negative-MAE scoring, a positive score drop corresponds to increased MAE in target units.')],
  'A tree ensemble may rank features by accumulated training impurity reduction. That answers a model-internal fitting question, not which variable causes demand. Features with many potential split values and correlated substitutes can complicate interpretation.\n\nPermutation importance asks a different question: how much does predictive score deteriorate when one feature’s values are shuffled on evaluation cases? Use development evaluation for interpretation while developing; do not repeatedly redesign features from final-test importance results and preserve an untouched-test claim. Correlated features can substitute for each other, so a small permutation effect is not proof of irrelevance.\n\nThe example uses the separate fictional development split. It reports both quantities with their definitions. Impurity values are dimensionless relative summaries, while the permutation score drop under negative MAE has cup-error meaning. Neither is a causal promotion effect or a universal feature ranking.',
  DEMAND_DEV + 'from sklearn.ensemble import RandomForestRegressor\nfrom sklearn.inspection import permutation_importance\nm = RandomForestRegressor(n_estimators=32, min_samples_leaf=3, random_state=45, n_jobs=1).fit(Xt, yt)\nr = permutation_importance(m, Xv, yv, n_repeats=3, random_state=45, scoring="neg_mean_absolute_error", n_jobs=1)\nfor name, impurity, drop in zip(X.columns, m.feature_importances_, r.importances_mean):\n    print(name, "training impurity", round(float(impurity), 3), "validation MAE increase", round(float(drop), 3))',
  'Check the sum of a fitted forest’s impurity importances on this nonconstant example.',
  DEMAND_DEV + 'from sklearn.ensemble import RandomForestRegressor\nm = RandomForestRegressor(n_estimators=8, random_state=45, n_jobs=1).fit(Xt, yt)\nprint("Importance sum:", round(float(m.feature_importances_.sum()), 6))',
  'Interpret a score change from -12 to -20 under negative-MAE scoring.',
  'original_score, shuffled_score = -12.0, -20.0\nprint("Score drop:", original_score - shuffled_score)\nprint("MAE increased by8cups in this illustration")',
  [('Does a large feature_importances_ value prove a causal effect?', 'No', 'Always', 'Only for promotion', 'It describes how this fitted model used training splits.'),
   ('Can correlated features hide one another’s permutation importance?', 'Yes', 'Never', 'Only when scores are negative', 'One feature may retain substitute information while the other is shuffled.')],
  'Call the highest importance a proven cause and remove all low-importance variables without evaluation.', 'State the importance definition, evaluated sample and correlation limitations.',
  'The training and validation summaries answer different questions and use different units. The permutation result is a score degradation on the same development cases, not a new fitted coefficient.')

L(4, '6. Compare linear and tree methods under one protocol',
  'Use identical data and metrics and distinguish a development ranking from a final claim.',
  [('Fair model comparison', 'Hold the evaluated cases, available features, primary criterion and fitting boundary fixed while comparing declared candidates.'),
   ('make_candidates', 'The project helper constructs fresh predeclared pipelines: four candidate models plus a separately named median baseline. Fresh objects prevent shared fitted state between experiments.')],
  'Compare different method families because they express different relationships. Linear regression has a global additive equation. A tree partitions regions. A forest averages varied trees. Boosting builds additive corrections. Their complexity and extrapolation behaviours differ, so choose with relevant development evidence rather than a popularity ranking.\n\nThe example compares four familiar methods on one separate development split. It is a readable first comparison, not the final model-selection protocol. Day6 compares the project’s four predeclared pipelines using the same five training-only folds, then reserves the final test for reporting. The project includes engineered Ridge instead of the single tree as one of those four candidates.\n\nIf no candidate improves on an appropriate baseline, a learned method is not automatically justified. Keep the baseline or reconsider the task and data. A tiny score difference should not outweigh complexity, data quality or evidence limits. Record which comparison produced a choice and do not silently swap the evaluation set or metric.',
  DEMAND_DEV + 'from sklearn.linear_model import LinearRegression\nfrom sklearn.tree import DecisionTreeRegressor\nfrom sklearn.ensemble import RandomForestRegressor, GradientBoostingRegressor\nfrom sklearn.metrics import mean_absolute_error\nmethods = {"linear": LinearRegression(), "tree": DecisionTreeRegressor(max_depth=5, min_samples_leaf=3, random_state=45), "forest": RandomForestRegressor(n_estimators=32, min_samples_leaf=3, random_state=45, n_jobs=1), "boosting": GradientBoostingRegressor(n_estimators=60, max_depth=2, random_state=45)}\nfor name, model in methods.items():\n    model.fit(Xt, yt)\n    print(name, "validation MAE", round(mean_absolute_error(yv, model.predict(Xv)), 3))',
  'List the final project’s candidate and baseline names without fitting or inspecting the test.',
  'from lab import make_candidates\nmodels = make_candidates()\nprint("Candidates:", [name for name in models if name != "baseline"])\nprint("Separate reference:", "baseline" in models)',
  'Choose the smaller development MAE and state its evidence boundary.',
  'scores = {"simple": 15.0, "complex": 14.8}\nwinner = min(scores, key=scores.get)\nprint("Development winner:", winner)\nprint("Small observed difference; final evaluation and complexity still matter")',
  [('Should candidates be scored on different convenient evaluation rows?', 'No', 'Yes', 'Only for trees', 'Changing the cases confounds the comparison.'),
   ('Does the best development score establish real-world superiority?', 'No; scope and independent evaluation still matter', 'Always', 'Only with four models', 'Selection evidence is limited to its data and protocol.')],
  'Compare a training score from one model with a held-out score from another.', 'Fit and evaluate each candidate under the same declared protocol.',
  'Each method receives Xt/yt and predicts exactly Xv. The printed MAE ranking is therefore an interpretable development comparison on these fictional cases, not a universal method ranking.')

L(5, '1. Freeze the independent-event demand question and holdout',
  'Rebuild the final project contract and verify the reserved row identities.',
  [('Independent-event assumption', 'The simulator generates separate fictional events. A shuffled row holdout is used under that assumption; repeated venues or a real future-day time series require a different group/time design.'),
   ('Regression splitting versus class stratification', 'Do not pass ordinary numerical target values directly as stratify=y as though each distinct amount were a classification label. Use a split design matched to the independent, grouped or chronological prediction setting.'),
   ('Frozen holdout contract', 'Reserve the final72 rows before model development. No data-dependent fitting, candidate selection or metric choice may use their answers.')],
  'The final project estimates later cup demand from registered guests, planned hours, a Celsius forecast and a planned promotion flag. All four inputs are available before the event. The target comes afterward. The dataset is generated by the author and includes noise, interactions and curvature intentionally.\n\nReserve twenty percent of360 rows for final reporting. The remaining288 rows support the entire model-comparison procedure. Keep row ids for traceability, not as inputs. Unlike last week’s small three-way example, this project uses training-only cross-validation for development and a separate final holdout.\n\nThis split matches the simulator’s independent-event design only. If a real project predicts future days, use a time-respecting protocol. If several events share a venue or customer and the goal is new-source prediction, consider grouping. Do not describe a shuffled synthetic row split as proof of real forecasting accuracy. The known simulator is a teaching device, not a deployed demand model.',
  'from lab import make_splits, FEATURES\nd = make_splits()\nprint("Inputs:", FEATURES)\nprint("Training/final rows:", len(d["X_train"]), len(d["X_test"]))\nprint("Row overlap:", len(set(d["X_train"].index) & set(d["X_test"].index)))\nprint("Training X/y paired:", d["X_train"].index.equals(d["y_train"].index))',
  'Verify complete row coverage without looking at held-out target values.',
  'from lab import make_splits\nd = make_splits()\nids = set(d["X_train"].index) | set(d["X_test"].index)\nprint("Complete360row coverage:", ids == set(range(360)))',
  'Choose a split design for a real future-day series and for new-venue prediction.',
  'plans = {"future days": "chronological evaluation", "new venues": "group by venue"}\nfor task, protocol in plans.items():\n    print(task + ": " + protocol)',
  [('Why does this lab use a shuffled row split?', 'Its declared rows are independent fictional events', 'Every demand problem should shuffle time', 'It guarantees high scores', 'The protocol follows the simulated collection design, not the word demand.'),
   ('May final labels guide the candidate list?', 'No', 'Yes if they are not passed to fit', 'Only for a baseline', 'Human choices based on held-out answers can contaminate selection.')],
  'Treat all demand datasets as independent shuffled rows.', 'Match independence and time boundaries to the actual prediction setting.',
  'The empty identity intersection and paired training index verify basic partition mechanics. The simulator assumption remains a declared scope condition, not something those checks prove about real events.')

L(5, '2. Keep every fitted preparation step inside each CV fold',
  'Trace positional fold indices and fit a fresh complete pipeline per fold.',
  [('cross_validate; multiple scorers', 'cross_validate fits fresh estimator clones on each training fold and can report several held-out scores. Its test_* fields mean the CV validation fold, not the project’s reserved final test.'),
   ('Fold-local preparation; make_folds', 'make_folds returns the same five KFold position pairs for the project training table. Each pipeline learns imputation and scaling only from its current fit positions.')],
  'Cross-validation rotates a validation boundary through the training portion. For each fold, fit a fresh pipeline on the allowed positions and evaluate on that fold’s held-out positions. The final project test is outside all these folds.\n\nA common mistake is fitting a scaler or feature selector on all training rows before cross-validation. That lets a fold’s validation distribution influence its preparation. Put fitted preparation inside the estimator passed to cross_validate so each fold has its own state. PolynomialFeatures here has no learned target relationship, but its column representation still belongs in the same inference sequence.\n\nFold indices are positional within X_train, not original event ids. Use iloc for those positions and keep y paired. Five folds with288 rows need not have exactly the same size. Their score spread is descriptive variability across these folds, not a confidence interval and not a calibrated future prediction range.',
  'from lab import make_splits, make_folds, make_candidates\nfrom sklearn.base import clone\nfrom sklearn.metrics import mean_absolute_error\nd = make_splits()\nfit, valid = make_folds(d["X_train"])[0]\nm = clone(make_candidates()["engineered_ridge"]).fit(d["X_train"].iloc[fit], d["y_train"].iloc[fit])\np = m.predict(d["X_train"].iloc[valid])\nprint("Fold fit/validation rows:", len(fit), len(valid))\nprint("Scaler fit rows:", int(m.named_steps["scale"].n_samples_seen_))\nprint("Fold MAE:", round(mean_absolute_error(d["y_train"].iloc[valid], p), 3))',
  'Verify that every training position appears in exactly one validation fold.',
  'from lab import make_splits, make_folds\nd = make_splits()\npositions = [int(i) for _, valid in make_folds(d["X_train"]) for i in valid]\nprint("Validation visits:", len(positions))\nprint("Unique positions:", len(set(positions)))',
  'Confirm that no CV validation event id belongs to the final holdout.',
  'from lab import make_splits, make_folds\nd = make_splits()\nfinal_ids = set(d["X_test"].index)\noverlap = set()\nfor _, valid in make_folds(d["X_train"]):\n    overlap |= set(d["X_train"].iloc[valid].index) & final_ids\nprint("Final overlap:", len(overlap))',
  [('What does test_mae from cross_validate name here?', 'The current CV validation-fold score', 'The project’s reserved final holdout automatically', 'A training-only metric', 'The library field name does not redefine the outer project test boundary.'),
   ('Where should scaling fit during each CV fold?', 'Only that fold’s fitting rows', 'All training rows before CV', 'All360rows', 'Fold-local fitting prevents validation information entering learned preparation.')],
  'Scale all rows once, then cross-validate a regressor and call the pipeline leakage-safe.', 'Cross-validate the complete preparation-and-regression pipeline.',
  'clone creates an unfitted estimator with the same settings. The scaler count matches the fold fit size, and iloc preserves the correct position-to-target pairing.')

L(5, '3. Compare four pipelines and select by the declared metric',
  'Read shared-fold model results and choose without consulting final-test performance.',
  [('compare_models; select_winner', 'The lab compares four predeclared model pipelines plus a median reference on shared training-only folds. select_winner chooses the lowest mean candidate CV MAE, with name as a deterministic tie-break.'),
   ('Fold standard deviation', 'The reported sample standard deviation describes fold MAE spread. It is not a confidence interval, and a small difference between means may not establish a meaningful advantage.')],
  'The candidate list is linear regression, degree-two engineered Ridge, random forest and gradient boosting. A training-median dummy is also scored as a separate reference. The primary selection criterion is mean five-fold MAE in cups, chosen before final inspection. RMSE is reported as complementary evidence.\n\nAll candidates use identical folds and complete pipelines. This comparison changes full methods, so a gap between linear and engineered Ridge combines representation and penalty differences; only Day4’s controlled ablation isolated the feature change. Keep those explanations separate.\n\nRead both mean and fold spread, then consider simplicity and intended use. If a selected candidate does not improve on the baseline meaningfully, a learned deployment is not justified merely because one candidate ranked first. The final reporting step now refits the chosen candidate on the full288 training rows before evaluating the reserved72. It does not select from test scores.',
  'from lab import make_splits, compare_models, select_winner\nd = make_splits()\nr = compare_models(d["X_train"], d["y_train"])\nfor name, score in r.items():\n    print(name, "mean MAE", round(score["mean_mae"], 3), "fold SD", round(score["sd_mae"], 3), "mean RMSE", round(score["mean_rmse"], 3))\nprint("Selected candidate:", select_winner(r))\nprint("Final test not used for this comparison")',
  'Check that the comparison contains four candidate entries plus the reference.',
  'from lab import make_candidates\nnames = list(make_candidates())\nprint("Candidate count:", len([name for name in names if name != "baseline"]))\nprint("Baseline present:", "baseline" in names)',
  'Apply the declared tie-break to an invented equal-MAE comparison.',
  'from lab import select_winner\nr = {"baseline": {"mean_mae": 20.0}, "z_model": {"mean_mae": 10.0}, "a_model": {"mean_mae": 10.0}}\nprint("Tie-break choice:", select_winner(r))',
  [('Which score selects the final candidate?', 'Mean training-only CV MAE', 'Highest final test R-squared', 'Lowest training error', 'The primary development criterion was declared before final reporting.'),
   ('Is fold standard deviation a prediction interval?', 'No', 'Always', 'Only with five folds', 'It summarises variability across fold scores, not uncertainty for a future individual demand.')],
  'Pick the candidate with the best visible final-test score and describe it as CV selection.', 'Select from the declared training-only CV table, then perform final reporting.',
  'The printed table includes four candidates and one reference. The winner is selected only from candidate mean_mae values; the final test is not passed to either comparison or selection.')

L(5, '4. Evaluate once and inspect residuals',
  'Report final MAE, MSE, RMSE and R-squared alongside a training-fitted baseline.',
  [('run_experiment; final report', 'The lab helper compares candidates using training-only CV, refits the winner on full training rows and then evaluates the final holdout. Calling it inspects the final test.'),
   ('Actual-versus-predicted plot', 'Points near the diagonal have small error in original target units. A residual plot shows direction and structure but does not itself provide a prediction interval.')],
  'The method is now chosen. Fit it on the full training portion and report predictions for the reserved72 events. The helper also fits the median baseline using training targets and scores the same final rows. Every metric therefore has a clear denominator and input boundary.\n\nThe two-panel figure shows actual versus predicted demand and signed residuals versus prediction. Read units and the diagonal or zero reference before interpreting a pattern. Report difficult cases and error size rather than just a flattering R-squared. The target is simulated, so even a strong score cannot establish actual café forecasting performance.\n\nOnce these final results are read, the holdout is seen. Use the errors for diagnosis if useful, but a revised model needs fresh evaluation for a new performance claim. Repeating this exact experiment demonstrates reproducibility. It does not give a second independent test, nor does the residual scatter calibrate a future demand interval.',
  'from lab import run_experiment\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nm, d, r = run_experiment()\npredicted = m.predict(d["X_test"])\nactual = d["y_test"].to_numpy()\nprint("Chosen by CV:", r["winner"])\nprint("Final events:", r["test_rows"])\nfor name in ["test_metrics", "baseline_test_metrics"]:\n    print(name, {key: round(value, 3) for key, value in r[name].items()})\nfig, axes = plt.subplots(1, 2, figsize=(8, 3.7))\naxes[0].scatter(actual, predicted, s=16, color="#376ea6")\nlow, high = min(actual.min(), predicted.min()), max(actual.max(), predicted.max())\naxes[0].plot([low, high], [low, high], linestyle="--", color="#777777")\naxes[0].set(xlabel="Actual cups", ylabel="Predicted cups", title="Final simulated events")\naxes[1].scatter(predicted, actual - predicted, s=16, color="#3d927a")\naxes[1].axhline(0, color="#777777", linewidth=1)\naxes[1].set(xlabel="Predicted cups", ylabel="Actual minus predicted (cups)", title="Signed final residuals")\nfig.tight_layout()\nfig.savefig("chart.png", dpi=110)\nplt.close(fig)',
  'Recalculate final MAE directly from the paired predictions.',
  'import numpy as np\nfrom lab import run_experiment\nm, d, r = run_experiment()\nmanual = float(np.abs(d["y_test"].to_numpy() - m.predict(d["X_test"])).mean())\nprint("Manual MAE:", round(manual, 6))\nprint("Reported MAE:", round(r["test_metrics"]["mae"], 6))',
  'Verify the RMSE-squared/MSE relationship from the final report.',
  'from lab import run_experiment\n_, _, r = run_experiment()\ns = r["test_metrics"]\nprint("MSE:", round(s["mse"], 6))\nprint("RMSE squared:", round(s["rmse"] ** 2, 6))',
  [('What data fits the reported median baseline?', 'Training targets only', 'Final holdout targets', 'The chosen predictions', 'The reference must obey the same training/evaluation boundary.'),
   ('Does this synthetic final score establish real-world forecast accuracy?', 'No', 'Always', 'Only if R-squared is high', 'The simulator’s scope limits the external claim regardless of score.')],
  'Retune after reading these errors and keep calling the same72events an untouched final test.', 'Report this frozen result and use fresh evaluation for later revised claims.',
  'Each metric uses the same final actual/predicted pairs. The baseline is scored on those identical cases. The residual sign remains actual minus predicted, consistent with Day1.',
  figure=('Final simulated event demand: actual versus predicted values and signed residuals', 'All72 final events are included. Axes use cups, and residuals are actual minus predicted. The scatter is diagnostic evidence, not a future prediction interval or proof of real customer performance.'))

L(5, '5. Predict a new event with a clear input contract',
  'Validate named inputs and separate a numerical estimate from a stock decision.',
  [('predict_checked; semantic units', 'The supplied interface requires exactly four named numeric columns, restores their order and validates complete finite values, guest counts, positive hours, Celsius range and a0/1 promotion flag.'),
   ('Planning versus prediction', 'A raw cup estimate is not a guaranteed integer stock requirement. Capacity, shortage cost, uncertainty and rounding rules belong to a separately evaluated decision procedure.')],
  'Create a named DataFrame for a fictional event. The wrapper keeps the row identity and restores the reviewed feature order before using the fitted pipeline. It rejects structural and obvious unit problems rather than quietly fitting on a caller’s new batch.\n\nThe interface requires complete inputs even though the training pipeline contains a median imputer. These are different policies: the imputer demonstrates fold-safe preparation, while the supplied prediction interface asks callers to provide the four known event inputs. If a real product allows missing forecasts, design and evaluate that policy explicitly.\n\nBroad numeric checks do not establish training support. A positive thousand-hour event could pass a basic positive-duration rule while being far outside this simulator’s one-to-five-hour training range. Likewise a plausible but wrong unit may evade a range check. Document support and scope. The raw model is not constrained to integer or nonnegative outputs, so do not claim every possible caller input produces a valid inventory plan.',
  'import pandas as pd\nfrom lab import make_splits, make_candidates, predict_checked, FEATURES\nd = make_splits()\nm = make_candidates()["engineered_ridge"].fit(d["X_train"], d["y_train"])\nnew = pd.DataFrame([[100, 3.0, 20.0, 1]], columns=FEATURES, index=["demo event"])\nprint("Raw estimate:", predict_checked(m, new).round(2).to_dict())\nprint("Estimate in cups; not an independently verified future outcome")',
  'Supply forecast100 and show the interface’s clear Celsius-range error.',
  'from lab import make_splits, make_candidates, predict_checked\nd = make_splits()\nm = make_candidates()["linear"].fit(d["X_train"], d["y_train"])\nrow = d["X_train"].iloc[:1].copy()\nrow["forecast_c"] = 100.0\ntry:\n    predict_checked(m, row)\nexcept ValueError as error:\n    print(str(error))',
  'Show that reversing named column order gives the same checked estimate.',
  'from lab import make_splits, make_candidates, predict_checked, FEATURES\nd = make_splits()\nm = make_candidates()["engineered_ridge"].fit(d["X_train"], d["y_train"])\nrow = d["X_train"].iloc[:1]\nprint("Order repaired:", predict_checked(m, row).equals(predict_checked(m, row[FEATURES[::-1]])))',
  [('Does basic input validation prove the request lies inside training support?', 'No', 'Always', 'Only for four features', 'Structural validity and relevance to observed data are different requirements.'),
   ('Is a raw fractional estimate automatically an evaluated inventory policy?', 'No', 'Always', 'Only if rounded upward', 'Decision rules need their own declared costs, constraints and evidence.')],
  'Treat any returned number as a guaranteed feasible stock order.', 'State input units, training support and the distinction between an estimate and a decision.',
  'The example fits a predeclared demonstration pipeline on training only and does not use a real event label. predict_checked returns a raw estimate while preserving the caller’s named row.')

L(5, '6. Milestone: a defensible offline demand experiment',
  'Deliver traceable artifacts and explain the model, metrics, comparison and limits.',
  [('write_report; reproducible artifacts', 'The final lab command writes a JSON report and a CSV of final actual/predicted pairs. These connect the selection protocol and headline metrics to inspectable evaluated rows.'),
   ('Evidence boundary', 'A repeatable simulator experiment demonstrates implementation and learning concepts. Real performance claims require relevant independent observed data and an appropriate evaluation design.')],
  'Run python lab.py --output artifacts from docs/python-week45 after installation. Read report.json and test-predictions.csv. The report records the simulated-data status, feature names, seed, versions, shared-fold CV scores, selection criterion, chosen method, baseline, final metrics and limitations. The CSV preserves all72 final event ids and residuals.\n\nWrite a conclusion in your own words. Explain a linear coefficient with its unit; distinguish MAE, MSE, RMSE and R-squared; describe polynomial overfitting and how Ridge/Lasso differ; show the feature-only ablation; compare the declared model pipelines; and state why the final test did not guide selection. Include what the synthetic setup cannot establish.\n\nBefore marking the last part complete, rebuild a small regression pipeline and compute one metric by hand. Solve the exercises and mini challenges before revealing their solutions. Use the existing notes, quizzes, Previous/Next controls and progress indicators as checkpoints. More study sessions are welcome: understanding matters more than finishing a nominal day. Week46 continues with classification rather than numerical amounts.',
  'import json\nimport pandas as pd\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom lab import write_report\nr = write_report("artifacts")\nsaved = json.loads(Path("artifacts/report.json").read_text(encoding="utf-8"))\nrows = pd.read_csv("artifacts/test-predictions.csv")\nprint("Chosen method:", saved["winner"])\nprint("Final CSV rows:", len(rows))\nprint("CSV MAE:", round(float(rows.residual.abs().mean()), 6))\nprint("JSON MAE:", round(r["test_metrics"]["mae"], 6))\nprint("Recorded limitations:", len(saved["limitations"]))',
  'Recalculate RMSE from the saved prediction CSV.',
  'import numpy as np\nimport pandas as pd\nfrom lab import write_report\nr = write_report("artifacts")\nrows = pd.read_csv("artifacts/test-predictions.csv")\nprint("CSV RMSE:", round(float(np.sqrt((rows.residual ** 2).mean())), 6))\nprint("JSON RMSE:", round(r["test_metrics"]["rmse"], 6))',
  'Create a completion checklist that includes interpretation and evidence limits, not only a successful run.',
  'items = ["task and units", "linear coefficient interpretation", "four metric definitions", "complexity and regularisation", "feature ablation", "training-only model comparison", "final prediction pairs", "synthetic-data limitations"]\nfor number, item in enumerate(items, 1):\n    print(number, item)',
  [('What makes the final result reviewable?', 'Declared workflow plus traceable metrics and prediction pairs', 'Only a large R-squared', 'Only the best screenshot', 'A reader must be able to connect the reported number to evaluated cases and decisions.'),
   ('Does repeating the same simulator run create independent real-world evidence?', 'No', 'Always', 'Only with a new output folder', 'Repetition verifies repeatability, not external performance.')],
  'Submit only the best model score without the source, protocol or limitations.', 'Submit the complete reproducible experiment and an honest explanation of its evidence.',
  'The CSV absolute residual mean reconstructs the JSON MAE. This final check ties the headline metric to the actual evaluated prediction pairs rather than an untraceable printed claim.',
  shell='python lab.py --output artifacts\npython -m pytest -q\npython -m ruff check .\npython -m ruff format --check .')
